# OpenTDF TDF3 Rust SDK

`opentdf-tdf3` is a Cargo library that executes the generated shared Go TDF3
implementation. Production calls need no compiler, Go process or stock SDK.
The supplied Cargo.lock pins native crypto, verified HTTPS and all dependencies.
The tested platform is Rust 1.98.0 on x86_64 Linux GNU. Dependency metadata
requires Rust 1.88 or later. Native builds need a C compiler, CMake, Make and Perl.
Vendored OpenSSL and AWS-LC are built from the pinned crate archives.

```rust,no_run
use opentdf_tdf3::{Config, EncryptOptions, CallOptions, encrypt, decrypt};
let config = Config {
    platform_url: "https://platform.example".into(),
    kas_url: "https://platform.example/kas".into(),
    issuer_url: "https://issuer.example".into(),
    client_id: "server-client".into(),
    client_secret: "read-from-server-storage".into(),
    ..Default::default()
};
let archive = encrypt(config.clone(), vec![0, 255],
    EncryptOptions { include_metadata: true, metadata: vec![], ..Default::default() },
    CallOptions::default()).wait()?;
let result = decrypt(config, archive, CallOptions::default()).wait()?;
assert_eq!(result.Payload, vec![0, 255]);
assert!(result.HasMetadata && result.Metadata.is_empty());
# Ok::<(), opentdf_tdf3::LibraryError>(())
```

`encrypt` and `decrypt` consume owned configuration, byte vectors and options
before submission. Inputs contain no borrowed source references. Nested routes,
strings and vectors move into the operation; native results own their bytes.
Rust String inputs are UTF-8; the generic compiler byte-string boundary preserves
arbitrary Go string bytes separately. Exact signed int64 values are retained.
Absent metadata and explicitly empty metadata differ through include_metadata.
Results expose Payload, Metadata, HasMetadata and ManifestJSON byte fields.

Operation implements Future<Output=Result<T, LibraryError>> and also has wait(),
cancel() and cancellation(). Calls safely serialize initialization, execution
and retirement. Each acquired call owns a fresh 16 MiB source thread. Native
values and errors detach on that owner, then resource retirement and thread
join/TLS teardown finish before publication. This does not require an async
executor when wait() is used; awaiting works in a native executor.

Queued cancellation releases queued inputs before settlement without waiting
for an active call. Active cancellation cancels source contexts and native
transport/provider work; settlement waits for actual cleanup. Dropping an
Operation requests stop and lets its owned worker finish retirement. Drop does
not acknowledge cleanup; retain and wait/await when a release acknowledgment is
needed. Cancellation after committed source success does not revoke that result.
CPU/native crypto completes before its next source cancellation boundary. Threads
are never forcibly terminated. Library entry does not replace process panic
hooks, change host thread/TLS policy, or install a global rustls crypto provider.

LibraryError distinguishes Source, Canceled, DeadlineExceeded, SourcePanic,
SourceFatal, HostFault and InvalidArgument. Its owned fields preserve declared
SDK Code, Operation, HTTPStatus, ServerCode, ServerMessage, RequiredObligations
and CauseCategory. text(name) and integer(name) inspect common fields. Failures
return no partial plaintext. Source panic formatting stays on the owner. The
host must use Rust panic=unwind; stack limits are bounded generated-source
checks, not recovery from native stack exhaustion or catastrophic native faults.

Use token_provider to bridge a native callback returning AccessToken. Insert it
under Config.token_provider_name in CallOptions.providers. The callback receives
owned ProviderRequest.payload and a Cancellation flag. It runs on a native
worker outside source locks. Return only after releasing resources, including
when canceled. A callback that never releases prevents safe settlement. An async
provider can drive its own executor inside that worker and await its cleanup.
ProviderError::Rejected is a declared provider failure; Fault or unwinding is a
typed host fault. Reentrant providers must not block waiting for another call to
this serialized library. DPoP requires an explicit matching auth_private_key_pem
and the token's scheme, exact expiry and confirmation_jkt binding.

Keys remain internal owner-associated native registry identities. PEM imports
are owned per operation and released on success, error and cancellation. Aliases
share Close state; Close prevents new acquisitions and follows completion of
held synchronous native snapshots. No private-material erasure guarantee is
made. Crypto parameters are RSA-2048 OAEP SHA1/MGF1SHA1, RS256, P-256 ECDH32,
ES256 P1363, AES256-GCM, HKDF/HMAC/SHA256, native constant-time MAC verification
and randomness. PEM, JWK and base64 inputs are strict and bounded.

HTTP uses dedicated reqwest/rustls transports, default verified roots and
hostname checks, GET/POST, no redirects, bounded request/response headers and
bodies, real monotonic deadlines and active cancellation. The native runtime and
sockets are released before ACK. OS resolver jobs may delay settlement until
they release. HTTP/1 parser buffering is independently bounded by maintained
Hyper, and accepted header bytes are limited to 64 KiB. Generic crypto byte
arguments are bounded to 64 MiB; shared TDF limits and segmentation remain in
the source implementation. Unsupported mandatory features fail explicitly.

Cargo.lock and dependencies.lock.json record exact dependency archive hashes,
licenses and declared MSRVs. All transitive and bundled native notices are under
licenses/ with separate package/version paths. Use cargo build --locked --offline
once the verified Cargo archive cache is populated. This package is not published.
